# Naming

## What, not how

Name a word for the outward effect, not the implementation. `allot` is
right; `dp+!` is wrong. `cmove>` (destination direction) is right; `<cmove`
(backward iteration) is wrong.

## Length and characters

- Spell names in full: `initialize`, `terminal`, `buffer` -- not `dspl`,
  `len`.
- Prefer the shorter equivalent of equal clarity: `bright` over `intense`,
  `enable` over `activate`, `on` / `off` / `go` in context.
- ANS guarantees 31 significant characters; stay portable.
- Safe characters: printable ASCII `!`…`~` (0x21-0x7E). A name that parses
  as a number is hidden by the parser (`2` always pushes 2; `2dup` is fine
  because it contains letters).
- Hex literals begin with `0` so they cannot collide with a defined name:
  `0ade`, not `ade`.

## Hyphenation is a smell

A hyphenated name often mixes two concepts. Factor them:

```forth
\ BAD -- 8 entries, no reuse
enable-left-motor      disable-left-motor
enable-right-motor     disable-right-motor
enable-left-solenoid   disable-left-solenoid   ...

\ GOOD -- 6 entries, composable in phrases
: left      ( -- )      ... ;
: right     ( -- )      ... ;
: motor     ( ... -- )  ... ;
: solenoid  ( ... -- )  ... ;
: on        ( a -- )    true  swap ! ;
: off       ( a -- )    false swap ! ;

\ usage:  left motor on
```

## Hyphens vs `create` names (Gforth tokenizer)

Gforth splits **hyphenated tokens** into separate words. If you already defined `create ed-a` or `constant lru-cap`, a new name like `ed-a-len` or `lru-node@` is **not** one identifier — the parser sees `ed-a` (or `lru`) and a tail (`-len`, `-node@`, …). Symptom: mysterious `undefined` / wrong word executed after edits that “looked” like one name.

**Prefer:**

- Short accessors without repeating the full `create` prefix: `ea-len`, `lrunk@`, `ed@` / `ed!` on `create edtbl`.
- `create` names that are **one** token and not reused as a prefix in longer hyphenated names (`edtbl`, `lrunk`, not `ed-a` then `ed-a-len`).

**Still apply** “hyphenation is a smell” for factoring — this section is the **parser** pitfall when a prefix is already a defined word.

## Numbers in names

Do not bundle a digit into a name. `1channel 2channel … 7channel` becomes
`1 channel`, `2 channel`: the digit is a stack argument, not part of the
identifier.

## Convention table

These shapes are widely shared (App E). Use them so a reader can guess a
word's role from its name.

| shape    | meaning                              | example   |
|----------|--------------------------------------|-----------|
| `?foo`   | execute conditionally                | `?dup`    |
| `foo?`   | return a boolean                     | `empty?`  |
| `>foo`   | convert to *foo*                     | `>body`   |
| `foo>`   | forward direction / end marker       | `cmove>`  |
| `<foo`   | from *foo* / start marker            | `<#`      |
| `.foo`   | print / display *foo*                | `.s`      |
| `foo!`   | store into *foo*                     | `cursor!` |
| `foo@`   | fetch from *foo*                     | `cursor@` |
| `'foo`   | address of *foo*                     | `'limit`  |
| `#foo`   | count of *foo*                       | `#chars`  |
| `/foo`   | bytes per *foo*                      | `/record` |
| `+foo`   | advance / enable                     | `+record` |
| `-foo`   | retreat / disable                    | `-record` |
| `(foo)`  | internal / primitive helper          | `(.)`     |
| `0foo`   | initialise *foo*                     | `0record` |

## Type prefixes (Gforth manual)

Similar operations on different types use different names — Forth does not overload:

| Prefix | Type |
|--------|------|
| (none) | signed cell |
| `u` | unsigned cell |
| `c` | character |
| `d` / `2` | double-cell / two cells |
| `m` / `um` | mixed single/double |
| `f` | floating-point |

When signed and unsigned behave identically (e.g., `+`), only the prefix-less form exists. No compile-time type checking — wrong prefix means silent misinterpretation.

For the full typing model (contracts, domain shapes), see **`forth-stack.mdc`** § Typing mental model.

## Structure names (Gforth manual §5.22)

Avoid generic field names (`counter`, `next`) at top level — prefix with the structure:

| shape | example |
|-------|---------|
| `struct%` | `list%`, `point%` — structure descriptor |
| `struct-field` | `list-next`, `point-x` — field accessor |

Extended structures may reuse a base field name (`list-next` inside `intlist%`, not `intlist-next`).
